	spec: "On the web, typing a slash command or path shows its completions as a list above the prompt, like Slack's (each row: name, and a quieter description line; the list is drawn in the prompt box's own colour like a card, on the canvas, outlined with a lit left bar, the selected row solid in that colour with canvas-coloured text, never neutral grey or slate), without pressing Tab (phones have none). Tap or click a row to take it; Up/Down and Enter pick on a keyboard; Escape or typing past the choices hides it. It uses the same completion source as the terminal (common/completion). Replaces the notice line that listed choices. The menu is a stable overlay anchored to the top of the entry, above the textarea and send controls: opening it covers the statusline beneath rather than inserting a row or moving the statusline, transcript, input, or footer; it stays within the phone and desktop viewport and remains tappable. A keystroke never clears visible choices merely to wait for the host. A longer prefix filters already-known candidates immediately when possible; a host answer confirms or changes them. If the menu contents and selection are unchanged, keep the same menu and row DOM identity and the composer at the same position, even during a pending request and after its identical reply. Outdated replies never roll the menu back; genuine dismissal (Escape, send, non-completable text) still closes it. Browser tests check the fixed statusline and composer geometry as the menu opens and closes, the menu overlays the line and remains tappable at phone and desktop sizes, and unchanged replies make no DOM mutation.",
